\description{
\code{geom_pmf_2d()} evaluates a bivariate probability mass function on a
discrete lattice and renders it either as a balloon plot of points
(\code{type = "point"}, the default), with size encoding the probability mass,
or as a heatmap of tiles (\code{type = "tile"}), with fill encoding the
probability mass. One or more highest density regions can be highlighted
via \code{shade_hdr}: each lattice point is assigned the smallest requested HDR
that contains it, and the assignment is mapped to \code{alpha} as an ordered
factor, mirroring the legend convention of \code{\link[=geom_pdf_2d]{geom_pdf_2d()}}.
}
\details{
The supplied mass function uses ggfunction's 2D function convention, shared
with \code{\link[=geom_pdf_2d]{geom_pdf_2d()}} and \code{\link[=geom_function_2d_1d]{geom_function_2d_1d()}}: \code{fun} receives a single
numeric vector \code{v = c(x, y)} and returns one probability mass.

The evaluation lattice is the product of the per-axis supports: integers
spanning \code{xlim} and \code{ylim} (each defaulting to \code{0:10}), or the exact values
in \code{support_x} and \code{support_y} when provided. Distributions with
non-product support, such as a trinomial whose support is a simplex, can be
plotted by evaluating over a bounding lattice and returning \code{0} off the
support; cells with zero mass are removed by default (\code{drop_zeros = TRUE}).

The total mass over the lattice is checked to be 1 (within \code{1e-2}), with a
\code{\link[cli:cli_alert]{cli::cli_alert()}} otherwise; disable via
\code{options(ggfunction.check = FALSE)}. As in \code{\link[=geom_pmf]{geom_pmf()}}, the exact
\code{shade_hdr} coverages may not be achievable for a discrete distribution, in
which case the smallest HDR with coverage at least each target is used and
a message reports the actual coverages.

Unlike \code{\link[=geom_pmf]{geom_pmf()}}, \code{geom_pmf_2d()} defaults to \code{inherit.aes = FALSE}
since the layer is driven entirely by \code{fun}. For the default point mode,
\code{\link[ggplot2:scale_size]{ggplot2::scale_size_area()}} is recommended so that mass is proportional to
point area and zero mass maps to zero area.
}
\section{Computed variables}{

These are calculated by the \code{stat} part of the layer and can be accessed
with \code{\link[ggplot2:aes_eval]{ggplot2::after_stat()}}.
\describe{
\item{\code{after_stat(x)} and \code{after_stat(y)}}{Lattice coordinates.}
\item{\code{after_stat(prob)}}{Probability mass at each lattice point.}
\item{\code{after_stat(probs)}}{Only when \code{shade_hdr} is supplied: the
smallest requested HDR containing each lattice point, as an ordered
factor whose outermost level (e.g. \code{">95\%"}) collects points outside all
requested regions.}
}
}

\section{Aesthetics}{

Point mode understands the aesthetics of \code{\link[ggplot2:geom_point]{ggplot2::geom_point()}} (notably
\code{size}, \code{colour}, \code{fill}, \code{shape}, \code{alpha}, \code{stroke}); tile mode those of
\code{\link[ggplot2:geom_tile]{ggplot2::geom_tile()}} (notably \code{fill}, \code{alpha}, \code{colour}, \code{linewidth},
\code{width}, \code{height}). Points use the fillable shape 21 by default, with
\code{fill} following \code{colour} when unset, so mapping \code{fill} (e.g.
\code{fill = after_stat(probs)}) colors the point interiors while \code{colour}
outlines them. The probability mass is mapped to \code{size} (point) or \code{fill}
(tile) by default, and when \code{shade_hdr} is supplied, \code{alpha} is
additionally mapped to \code{after_stat(probs)}.
}

\examples{
# Independent product binomial
dbinom2 <- function(v, sizes = c(10, 10), probs = c(0.5, 0.5)) {
  dbinom(v[1], sizes[1], probs[1]) * dbinom(v[2], sizes[2], probs[2])
}

ggplot() +
  geom_pmf_2d(fun = dbinom2, xlim = c(0, 10), ylim = c(0, 10)) +
  scale_size_area()

# Parameterized via `args`
ggplot() +
  geom_pmf_2d(
    fun = dbinom2, xlim = c(0, 10), ylim = c(0, 10),
    args = list(probs = c(0.3, 0.7))
  ) +
  scale_size_area()

# Tile heatmap
ggplot() +
  geom_pmf_2d(fun = dbinom2, xlim = c(0, 10), ylim = c(0, 10),
    type = "tile")

# Highlight the 50/80/95\% highest density regions
ggplot() +
  geom_pmf_2d(fun = dbinom2, xlim = c(0, 10), ylim = c(0, 10),
    shade_hdr = c(0.5, 0.8, 0.95)) +
  scale_size_area()

# Non-product support: trinomial over a bounding lattice
dtrinom <- function(v, size = 8, prob = c(0.3, 0.3, 0.4)) {
  if (sum(v) > size) return(0)
  dmultinom(c(v, size - sum(v)), prob = prob)
}

ggplot() +
  geom_pmf_2d(fun = dtrinom, xlim = c(0, 8), ylim = c(0, 8)) +
  scale_size_area() +
  coord_equal()

}
